  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 AM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nThe weather has been particularly boring as of late. This dry \nand warm pattern will continue for the first half of this week \nbefore the ridging starts to see ripples in the flow. The first \nripple will bring a weak front to dip down near central IL on \nThursday, then retreat back north Friday, possibly bringing some\nlight rain north of I-72. However, it could pass without any \nactivity due to how dry our airmass is. As of now, there is a \n10-30% chance of showers for Thursday into Friday.\n\nA second, stronger disturbance will follow closely behind this \nweekend, bringing a 30-50% chance for showers Saturday into Sunday. \nAmounts are forecast to be minimal, but there is time for this \nforecast to change. The global models do not exactly agree on timing \nor strength of the weekend/next week system. The GFS has a less \ndefined disturbance pass through over the weekend. The Euro model \nhas a much stronger, defined low pressure system and cold front \nforecast for next Monday. Timing/location/amounts will continue \nto shift as we move closer. Trends will continue to be monitored\nwith future forecasts. Overall, it looks like we may be seeing \na more active pattern develop late this week.\n\nTemperatures will continue to be above normal. Highs will be in the \nmid to upper 70s, nearing 80 in some places later in the week. Lows \nwill be in the 50s. Temperatures will trend slightly cooler starting \nthis weekend into next week. \"Cooler\" in this sense means \ntemperatures will be in the low 70s for highs. Normals for this time \nof year is around 61-65 degrees across central and southeastern IL. \nThe CPC's 6-10 day, 8-14 day, and Week 3-4 outlooks all show above \nnormal temperatures. Dry weather will last through much of the \nextended period as well.",
